.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 3h8l4 4v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H6a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM14 3v4h4M9 17l6-6"/><circle cx="9.5" cy="11.5" r="1"/><circle cx="14.5" cy="16.5" r="1"/></svg></span><span><strong>To change the rates</strong><span class="t">Two-thirds of each house</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></span><span><strong>Next reassessment</strong><span class="t">2029, our estimate</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Chief Operating Officer, and <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Abdulla Hijazi</strong>, Chief Marketing Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 7, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">A Myrtle Beach second home or rental is taxed on 6% of its value, plus school operating tax. A home you live in pays on 4%, without that tax.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No, not on his own. Lowering the 6% rate on second homes and rentals takes two-thirds of each house of the legislature, and no bill we found would lower it.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Alan Wilson, who won the governor's race, plans to look at reforming Act 388. Republicans won 79 of the 124 state House seats in the unofficial count, four short of two-thirds.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a home you live in, nothing pending raises the 4% rate. The one bill on that rate signed this year keeps it for heirs for a time.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Buying on the Grand Strand? We work out the tax bill on the home first.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Get the tax on a home</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#what-act-388-did" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What Act 388 did</a></li><li><a href="#why-second-homes-pay-more" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Why second homes pay more</a></li><li><a href="#the-15-cap" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">The 15% cap and a sale</a></li><li><a href="#candidates" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What the candidates said</a></li><li><a href="#bills" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Bills and their status</a></li><li><a href="#what-it-would-take" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What it would take</a></li><li><a href="#next-reassessment" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Next reassessment</a></li><li><a href="#who-pays" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Who pays more or less</a></li><li><a href="#evidence" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Evidence on both sides</a></li><li><a href="#short-and-long-run" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Short and long run</a></li><li><a href="#act-388-questions" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Act 388 questions</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-act-388-did"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What Act 388 did">What is Act 388 in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Act 388 is the 2006 state law that took school operating taxes off homes their owners live in. Everyone else still pays them, including second homes, rentals and businesses.</p><div class="a388-grid"><div class="a388-card"><h3>Homes their owners live in</h3><p>They stopped paying the school operating tax. They still pay the school debt tax, the county's tax and the city's.</p></div><div class="a388-card"><h3>An extra 1% sales tax</h3><p>The state sales tax rose by 1%, but not on hotel and rental stays. The money goes to a separate fund that pays school districts back.</p></div><div class="a388-card"><h3>The 15% limit</h3><p>At each countywide reassessment, a property's taxable value can rise no more than 15% over five years. It took effect after a change to the state constitution was ratified.</p></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In Horry County the school operating tax is the largest single part of a second home's bill.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What has changed since?</h3><div class="a388-tl" role="list" aria-label="Act 388 and the changes since, year by year"><div role="listitem" class="r"><span class="y">2006</span><span class="t"><strong>Act 388 passes.</strong> Homes their owners live in stop paying school operating tax.</span></div><div role="listitem" class="r"><span class="y">2007</span><span class="t"><strong>The 1% sales tax starts, and the change to the constitution behind the 15% limit is ratified.</strong></span></div><div role="listitem" class="r"><span class="y">2011</span><span class="t"><strong>Act No. 57.</strong> When a 6% property sells, 25% of its value at the sale is exempt.</span></div><div role="listitem" class="r"><span class="y">2019</span><span class="t"><strong>Horry County reassessment.</strong></span></div><div role="listitem" class="r"><span class="y">2024</span><span class="t"><strong>The next Horry County reassessment</strong>, as the county scheduled it.</span></div><div role="listitem" class="r"><span class="y">2026</span><span class="t"><strong>S. 815 is filed, and Act 140 is signed.</strong> Wilson wins the governor's race; Jermaine Johnson concedes.</span></div><div role="listitem" class="r next"><span class="y">2028</span><span class="t"><strong>The next general election.</strong> The earliest statewide vote on a change to the constitution.</span></div><div role="listitem" class="r next"><span class="y">2029</span><span class="t"><strong>Horry County's next reassessment</strong>, expected. Our estimate from the five-year rule.</span></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="why-second-homes-pay-more"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Why second homes pay more">Why does a second home or rental in Myrtle Beach pay more property tax?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The state constitution taxes a home you do not live in on 6% of its value instead of 4%, and it still pays the school operating tax. On the same house inside Myrtle Beach, that makes the second-home bill more than twice the homeowner's. Our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Horry County property tax guide</a> shows how each bill is figured, and our <a href="/invest/rental-property-tax-cut/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">page for rental investors works each bill at 6% and 4%</a>.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3