 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the 6% rate apply to a condo I rent out?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The 4% rate is only for the home the owner lives in and applies for. A condo, house or townhouse you rent out is taxed at 6%, whether it rents by the week or by the year."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Would a second home that is never rented get the cut?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lawmakers have not decided. Today the 6% rate covers every home its owner does not live in, rented or not. Lawmakers would decide which homes a change covers when they write the bill."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the 4% rate stay on a home after its owner dies?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For a time, under a 2026 law. The home keeps the 4% rate until the estate closes or a deed out of it is recorded. At the latest, it keeps it until December 31 of the year after the death. It loses the rate if it is rented for more than 72 days."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Would a cut change the taxes a short-term rental collects from guests?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The plan is about property tax. A weekly rental still collects the state's 7% accommodations tax and any local tax of up to 3%. A few rentals are exempt, such as a home rented for 14 days or fewer in a year."}}]}</script>
