 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/sell/capital-gains/#article","headline":"Capital Gains Tax When Selling a House in South Carolina, With the Nonresident Withholding Explained","description":"What you owe when selling South Carolina property in 2026: the federal 0/15/20 brackets, the primary residence exclusion, depreciation recapture, South Carolina's 44 percent gain deduction and new 5.21 percent top rate, and the I-290 withholding every out-of-state seller hits at closing, with a calculator.","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/sell/capital-gains/","author":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"reviewedBy":{"@type":"Person","name":"Timothy Nash","alternateName":"Tim Nash","jobTitle":"Broker-in-Charge"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"datePublished":"2026-07-20","dateModified":"2026-10-04","inLanguage":"en-US","image":"https://chapter3realty.com/og-image.jpg","mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/sell/capital-gains/#webpage"},"about":{"@type":"Thing","name":"South Carolina capital gains and nonresident withholding"},"keywords":"capital gains tax selling house South Carolina, SC nonresident withholding real estate, I-290 South Carolina, taxes selling rental property South Carolina, SC capital gains rate 2026"}</script>
 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/sell/capital-gains/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Does South Carolina tax capital gains on a home sale?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, but gently: South Carolina deducts 44 percent of a net long-term capital gain, so only 56 percent is taxed. At the new 5.21 percent top rate for 2026 that works out to an effective rate of about 2.92 percent on long-term gains, on top of whatever federal tax applies."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is South Carolina's income tax rate for 2026?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Two brackets under the law signed March 30, 2026: 1.99 percent on taxable income up to $30,000 and 5.21 percent above it. The 2025 top rate was 6.0 percent. Further automatic cuts can trigger from 2027 if state revenue targets are met."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much does South Carolina withhold when an out-of-state owner sells?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The buyer's side withholds at South Carolina's top individual rate for the year of the sale, 5.21 percent for 2026 closings, or 5 percent for corporations. With a signed Form I-295 gain affidavit, the rate applies only to your actual gain. Without one, it applies to the entire amount realized, capped at your net proceeds."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is the South Carolina withholding an extra tax?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. It is a prepayment. The withholding ignores the 44 percent gain deduction, so it nearly always over-collects; you claim the withheld amount as a credit on a South Carolina nonresident return and receive the difference back. Most long-term sellers are owed a refund."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I pay the withholding when selling my primary residence?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, if the gain is excluded under the federal Section 121 primary-residence exclusion, up to $250,000 single or $500,000 married. You certify the exclusion on Form I-295 and no withholding applies, even if you have already moved out of state, as long as you are within the exclusion's two-of-five-year window."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What if I inherit a house in South Carolina and sell it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Your basis steps up to the date-of-death value, so the taxable gain is usually small or zero. But the withholding still applies by default for nonresident heirs: without a gain affidavit, 5.21 percent of the whole sale price gets withheld and you wait for a refund. The I-295 affidavit certifying the near-zero gain is what prevents that; our inherited house guide covers the rest."}}]}</script>
