or="#1c2028"/><text class="l" x="0" y="-35" text-anchor="middle"><tspan x="0">Beach</tspan></text><text class="m" x="0" y="-19.5" text-anchor="middle">about 25 min</text></g></g><g transform="translate(212.2 312.1)"><g class="k"><rect x="-114.5" y="-24.9" width="101.5" height="52.5" rx="10" fill="#1c2028" fill-opacity=".1"/><rect x="-114.5" y="-26.2" width="101.5" height="52.5" rx="10" fill="#ffffff" fill-opacity=".9" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width=".8"/><circle r="11" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#91592b" stroke-width="1.6"/><use href="#c3r-downtown-conway-airport" x="-7.4" y="-7.4" width="14.7" height="14.7" color="#1c2028"/><text class="l" x="-15" y="-11.2" text-anchor="end"><tspan x="-15">Myrtle Beach</tspan><tspan x="-15" dy="16.5">airport</tspan></text><text class="m" x="-15" y="20.8" text-anchor="end">about 27 min</text></g></g><g transform="translate(85 130.2)"><g class="k"><rect x="9.9" y="-38.6" width="90.7" height="36" rx="10" fill="#1c2028" fill-opacity=".1"/><rect x="9.9" y="-39.9" width="90.7" height="36" rx="10" fill="#ffffff" fill-opacity=".9" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width=".8"/><circle r="11" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#91592b" stroke-width="1.6"/><use href="#c3r-downtown-conway-town" x="-7.4" y="-7.4" width="14.7" height="14.7" color="#1c2028"/><text class="l" x="11.9" y="-24.9"><tspan x="11.9">University</tspan></text><text class="m" x="11.9" y="-9.4">about 10 min</text></g></g><g transform="translate(113.4 145.9)"><g class="k"><rect x="-57.2" y="15.3" width="114.5" height="52.5" rx="10" fill="#1c2028" fill-opacity=".1"/><rect x="-57.2" y="14" width="114.5" height="52.5" rx="10" fill="#ffffff" fill-opacity=".9" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width=".8"/><circle r="11" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#91592b" stroke-width="1.6"/><use href="#c3r-downtown-conway-hospital" x="-7.4" y="-7.4" width="14.7" height="14.7" color="#1c2028"/><text class="l" x="0" y="29" text-anchor="middle"><tspan x="0">Conway</tspan><tspan x="0" dy="16.5">Medical Center</tspan></text><text class="m" x="0" y="61" text-anchor="middle">about 10 min</text></g></g><g transform="translate(45 65.2)"><g class="k"><g transform="scale(0.8)"><ellipse cx="0" cy="1" rx="7" ry="2.4" fill="#1c2028" opacity=".18"/><path d="M0 0C-3-6-13-14-13-26a13 13 0 1 1 26 0c0 12-10 20-13 26z" fill="#c4783a" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.5"/><use href="#c3r-downtown-conway-home" x="-8.5" y="-34.5" width="17" height="17" color="#ffffff"/></g><text class="h" x="15" y="-14.5"><tspan x="15">Downtown Conway</tspan></text></g></g></g></svg></div><figcaption>Drive times by car from downtown Conway, without traffic. Map data: U.S. Census Bureau TIGER/Line; Horry County GIS.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="city-or-county"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="City or county">Is a Conway address inside the city of Conway?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Usually not. Only a little more than 1 in 4 homes with a Conway mailing address is inside the city limits. The rest are in the county, outside any city.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Homes with a Conway mailing address, rounded"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Homes with a Conway mailing address, rounded</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Where the home is</span><span role="columnheader">Homes</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Inside the city limits</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.379" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">8,300</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Outside, in the county</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">22,000</span></span></div></div></div></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The same is true of homes for sale. At our last count, only about 1 in 4 houses for sale with a Conway address was inside the city.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What the city line changes</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Inside the city, the property tax is about $670 a year more on a typical house you live in. The City of Conway picks up garbage, recycling and yard waste at the curb once a week for homes in the city, and the city has its own police and fire departments.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Outside the city there is no city pickup. Homes in the county, outside any city, are the only ones allowed to use the county's 25 recycling and convenience centers, where owners bring their own trash.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Inside the city or not, for one address</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us a Conway address. We tell you which side of the city line it is on, and what that means for its tax and its trash pickup.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us look up an address</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="parts-of-town"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Five parts of town">Which part of Conway should you look at?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The city's homes fall into five parts. Each has something the others do not. The numbers on the map match the entries below. Each price is what a house or townhouse there usually resold for in the past year, from county deeds.</p><figure class="c3-cw-fig"><div class="c3-cw-frame"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-conway/conway-parts-map.svg" width="760" height="830" alt="Map of the city of Conway with its homes shown as dots, coloured by part of town and numbered: 1 downtown, by the Waccamaw River; 2 near the university, off US 501 to the southeast; 3 west and south of downtown, the largest; 4 around Crabtree Swamp, north and northwest of downtown; 5 the north edge. Coastal Carolina University and Conway Medical Center are in part 2." loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><figcaption>Map of the city's homes, each dot a group of homes, coloured by part of town. The dark square is Coastal Carolina University and the red cross is Conway Medical Center. Map data: Horry County GIS; U.S. Census Bureau TIGER/Line.</figcaption><ol class="c3-cw-key" aria-label="The five parts of Conway"><li><span class="c3-cw-num p-downtown" aria-hidden="true">1</span><a href="#downtown">Downtown</a></li><li><span class="c3-cw-num p-us501" aria-hidden="true">2</span><a href="#us-501">Near the university, off US 501</a></li><li><span class="c3-cw-num p-west" aria-hidden="true">3</span><a href="#west-and-south">West and south</a></li><li><span class="c3-cw-num p-crabtree" aria-hidden="true">4</span><a href="#crabtree-swamp">Around Crabtree Swamp</a></li><li><span class="c3-cw-num p-north" aria-hidden="true">5</span><a href="#north-edge">The north edge</a></li></ol></figure><h3 id="downtown" data-toc="Downtown" class="c3-cw-h3" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.3rem;color:var(--navy);margin:2rem 0 .6rem;line-height:1.3"><span class="c3-cw-num p-downtown" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span>Downtown: a walk to Main Street and the river</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Downtown is the old town on the river. Main Street, City Hall, the Riverwalk and Riverfront Park are all here. Kingston Park on Main Street has shaded tables and a fountain, and the Garden Walk off Third Avenue is a walkway with music and lights in the evening.</p><div class="hx-strip" data-place="conway-downtown" data-name="Downtown Conway"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/buyers/no-hoa/conway-older-house-and-oak-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/buyers/no-hoa/conway-older-house-and-oak-600w.webp 600w, /images/buyers/no-hoa/conway-older-house-and-oak-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="900" alt="An older white wood house with black shutters behind a large live oak, with a paved driveway, in downtown Conway" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Pubdog. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: Pubdog</a></span></div><figcaption>An older house under a live oak, in the downtown historic district.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Downtown here means the streets within about a mile of the center of town, on house lots of about a third of an acre. Snowhill, Mayfair, Buckwood, Highland Park and Collinswood are the largest subdivisions. Few homes here come up for sale. Those that did resold for about $275,000.</p><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=Downtown%20Conway">Get the homes for sale in Downtown Conway<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p><h3 id="us-501" data-toc="Near the university, off US 501" class="c3-cw-h3" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.3rem;color:var(--navy);margin:2rem 0 .6rem;line-height:1.3"><span class="c3-cw-num p-us501" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span>Near the university, off US 501: the university and the hospital</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Along US 501 toward the beach, the city reaches past Coastal Carolina University. Conway Medical Center, on Singleton Ridge Road, and the Conway campus of Horry-Georgetown Technical College are in this part too.</p><div class="hx-strip" data-place="conway-us-501" data-name="Conway near the university"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-conway/coastal-carolina-kimbel-library-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-conway/coastal-carolina-kimbel-library-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/submarkets-conway/coastal-carolina-kimbel-library-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="900" alt="A red brick library building with white doors behind a wide live oak with long low limbs, at Coastal Carolina University in Conway" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: LittleT889. License: CC BY-SA 4.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 4.0">Photo: LittleT889</a></span></div><figcaption>Kimbel Library at Coastal Carolina University, under a live oak.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Wild Wing, Coastal Point West, Kiskadee Parke and Aviary Village are the largest subdivisions, with about 1,430 homes in all, on lots of about four-tenths of an acre. Homes here resold for about $360,000. Only the north edge was higher.</p><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=Conway%20near%20the%20university">Get the homes for sale in Conway near the university<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p><h3 id="west-and-south" data-toc="West and south" class="c3-cw-h3" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.3rem;color:var(--navy);margin:2rem 0 .6rem;line-height:1.3"><span class="c3-cw-num p-west" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span>West and south: the most homes, three city parks and the lowest prices</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">West and south of downtown, toward US 378 and US 701 South, is the largest part of the city. The Billy Gardner Recreation Complex on Mill Pond Road holds the Conway Recreation Center, with an indoor pool and walking track, plus ball fields and a playground built for children of all abilities. Smith Jones Park has an outdoor pool, and Collins Park has tennis and pickleball courts.</p><div class="hx-strip" data-place="conway-west-and-south" data-name="West Conway"></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Rosehaven, New Castle, Oak Glenn, Woodland Lakes and Jasmine Woods are the largest subdivisions, on lots of about a quarter of an acre. Homes here resold for about $245,000, the lowest of the five parts.</p><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=West%20Conway">Get the homes for sale in West Conway<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p><h3 id="crabtree-swamp" data-toc="Around Crabtree Swamp" class="c3-cw-h3" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.3rem;color:var(--navy);margin:2rem 0 .6rem;line-height:1.3"><span class="c3-cw-num p-crabtree" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span>Around Crabtree Swamp: north and northwest of downtown, with a park on Sherwood Drive</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">North and northwest of downtown, around Crabtree Swamp, are about 2,390 homes, most of them 2 to 4 miles out. Sherwood Park on Sherwood Drive has a playground and a sand volleyball court, and across the street a nine-hole disc golf course inside a walking track.</p><div class="hx-strip" data-place="conway-crabtree-swamp" data-name="Conway near Crabtree Swamp"></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Midtown Village, Rivertown Row, Elmhurst, Woodcreek, Kingston Bay, Sherwood and Country Club Forest are the largest subdivisions. The lots are the smallest in the city, about a fifth of an acre, and homes resold for about $260,000.</p><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=Conway%20near%20Crabtree%20Swamp">Get the homes for sale in Conway near Crabtree Swamp<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p><h3 id="north-edge" data-toc="The north edge" class="c3-cw-h3" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.3rem;color:var(--navy);margin:2rem 0 .6rem;line-height:1.3"><span class="c3-cw-num p-north" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span>The north edge: the biggest lots in the city</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At the city's north edge, 4 to 5 miles from downtown, a typical lot is about three-quarters of an acre, the largest in the city. Ridgewood West, Country Manor and Maple Hill are the largest subdivisions, with about 560 homes in all.</p><div class="hx-strip" data-place="conway-north-edge" data-name="North Conway"></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Few homes resold here, and the middle price was about $400,000, the highest of the five parts.</p><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=North%20Conway">Get the homes for sale in North Conway<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="flooding"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Flooding">Does Conway, SC flood?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A few parts do. About 95 in 100 homes in the city are outside both FEMA's high-risk zone and the land Florence's water reached in 2018. The rest are near the river, Crabtree Swamp and Kingston Lake.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">By part of town, none of the homes near the university are in either flood map. Downtown and at the north edge, about 3 in 100 homes are in one. West and south, it is about 5 in 100, and around Crabtree Swamp about 1 in 10.</p><figure class="c3-cw-fig"><div class="c3-cw-frame"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-conway/conway-city-flood-map.svg" width="760" height="830" alt="Map of the city of Conway: dark dots are its homes; light blue is FEMA's high-risk flood zone and darker blue is where Florence's water reached in 2018. The blue lies along the Waccamaw River, Crabtree Swamp north of downtown around Sherwood, and Kingston Lake. Most dots, including all of those near the university, are outside it." loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><figcaption><ul class="c3-cw-legend" aria-label="Map colors"><li><span class="c3-cw-sw" style="background:#1c2028;border-radius:50%;width:.8rem;height:.8rem"></span>Homes</li><li><span class="c3-cw-sw" style="background:#8fb3c7"></span>FEMA's high-risk flood zone</li><li><span class="c3-cw-sw" style="background:#2f6f8f;opacity:.75"></span>Where Florence's water reached</li></ul>Map of the two flood maps under the city's homes. Map data: Horry County GIS; U.S. Census Bureau TIGER/Line.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The worst flood on record came after Hurricane Florence in 2018. The river gauge at the Conway Marina read about 21 feet, its highest ever. That is the height of the river on the gauge, not the depth of water on a street. Major flooding starts at 14 feet.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-10-d/buyers-undisclosed-flooding/conway-florence-floodwater-street-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-10-d/buyers-undisclosed-flooding/conway-florence-floodwater-street-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-10-d/buyers-undisclosed-flooding/conway-florence-floodwater-street-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="844" alt="Floodwater covering a street and the yards of single-story houses, with road signs standing in the water, in the Conway area after Hurricane Florence" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:PD-USGov-Military-National_Guard" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: SC National Guard. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: SC National Guard</a></span></div><figcaption>Floodwater on a street of houses in the Conway area after Florence.</figcaption></figure><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Why the flood comes late and stays</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The flood here comes down the river, days after the storm. Florence came ashore in North Carolina, and the river at Conway was highest 12 days later.</p><figure class="c3-gchart c3-cw-fig" style="max-width:640px"><span class="cap" style="display:block;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1.1em;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 .5rem">How high the river at Conway rose on its gauge, in feet, after two hurricanes</span><ul class="c3-cw-legend"><li><span class="c3-cw-line" style="background:#c4783a"></span>Florence, 2018</li><li><span class="c3-cw-line" style="background:#5c6b78"></span>Matthew, 2016</li><li><svg width="26" height="6" aria-hidden="true" style="flex:none"><line x1="0" y1="3" x2="26" y2="3" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".7" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="2 3"/></svg>Major flooding, 14 ft</li><li><svg width="26" height="6" aria-hidden="true" style="flex:none"><line x1="0" y1="3" x2="26" y2="3" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".7" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/></svg>Flood stage, 11 ft</li></ul><svg class="c3-cw-chart" viewBox="0 0 360 252" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="Line chart of the Waccamaw River's height on the gauge at Conway, by days after the storm came ashore. After Florence the river rose past flood stage (11 feet) on day 2, was highest, about 21 feet, on day 12, and stayed above flood stage for 29 days. After Matthew it was highest, about 18 feet, on day 10 and stayed above flood stage for 26 days."><rect x="34" y="8" width="318" height="204" fill="rgba(255,255,255,.55)" rx="6"/><rect x="34" y="8" width="318" height="113.3" fill="#2f6f8f" fill-opacity=".07"/><line x1="34" y1="155.3" x2="352" y2="155.3" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".6" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/><line x1="34" y1="121.3" x2="352" y2="121.3" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".6" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-dasharray="2 3"/><line x1="76" y1="8" x2="76" y2="212" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".35" stroke-width="1"/><path d="M34 179.8L40 179.9L46 180.3L52 180.3L58 179.8L64 179.8L70 178.5L